  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Question": "If \\(1<a<3\\), simplifying \\(\\sqrt{(a-1)^2}+\\sqrt{(a-3)^2}\\) gives a constant. What is this constant?",
  "Choices": {},
  "Correct Answer": "2",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "Since \\(a-1>0\\) and \\(a-3<0\\), the original expression equals \\((a-1)+(3-a)=2\\).\n\nAlternative method 1: Interpret the terms as distances\n\nUsing \\(\\sqrt{x^2}=|x|\\), the two terms are the distances from \\(a\\) to \\(1\\) and from \\(a\\) to \\(3\\) on the number line. Since \\(1<a<3\\), these distances are the lengths of two adjacent segments that together form the segment from \\(1\\) to \\(3\\). Their sum is therefore \\(3-1=2\\), so the constant is \\(2\\).",
